window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Three short paths. Do the one you need first; they work together or alone."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Hits (about two minutes)","u":"getting-started.html#1-hits-about-two-minutes","x":"Select an enemy, a prop or a folder of prefabs. Open Tools > Responsive FX > Hits > Setup Wizard , pick a preset, press Add Hit Feedback . Where your game deals damage, add one line: HitFeedback.Hit(target, 25f); // a hit for 25 HitFeedback.Hit(target, new HitInfo(60f, HitKind.Critical).At(hitPoint)); // a critical, at a point Your own health script keeps control of damage and death. See Hits ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Footsteps (about two minutes)","u":"getting-started.html#2-footsteps-about-two-minutes","x":"Select your character. Open Tools > Responsive FX > Steps > Setup Wizard and press Add Footsteps . Press Play and walk. The wizard guessed each material's surface from its name; change any guess in step 3 of the wizard, or later in the Surface Library. See Steps ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Contacts: bullets, bumps and anything else","u":"getting-started.html#3-contacts-bullets-bumps-and-anything-else","x":"A crate, a bottle, a barrel: add the Physics Contacts component to anything with a Rigidbody. It now thuds, slides, rolls and breaks on whatever it touches, with no code. A bullet or a ray: one line where your shot lands. if (Physics.Raycast(origin, direction, out var hit, range)) Contacts.PlayHit(hit); // the surface it hit answers: sound, effect, mark, noise for AI Anything else (a door slamming, a spell landing): Contacts.Play(ContactKind.Interaction, door, point, normal, intensity: 1.5f); See Contacts ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Make each surface yours","u":"getting-started.html#make-each-surface-yours","x":"Open Tools > Responsive FX > Impact Lab . The first tab is a grid: surfaces down the side, kinds of contact across the top. Click a cell to hear it and edit it. The samples already answer steps, hits, bullets, bumps, slides, rolls and breaks on wood, stone, metal, glass, grass, gravel, dirt, sand, snow, mud, leaves, carpet, water and ice. Press Make my own copy in the footstep wizard (or copy the sample library yourself) before you change the samples, so an update never overwrites your work."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Let players choose","u":"getting-started.html#let-players-choose","x":"Add a Feedback Options Panel to any object and call Show() from your menu. In Impact Lab's Players tab you decide which options players see and which are fixed. See Player options and screens ."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"When something does not play","u":"getting-started.html#when-something-does-not-play","x":"Impact Lab > Trace lists every contact while you play: what it hit, which rule decided the surface, what played and what was held back, with the reason in plain words. Why No Feedback? (hits) and Why No Footstep? (steps) check one object and offer a fix. Tools > Responsive FX > Diagnostics checks the whole scene."},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Impact Lab","u":"impact-lab.html#impact-lab","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Impact Lab is the main window. It has five tabs and a gear for the project settings. It works docked at any width: rows that do not fit scroll with an arrow at each end."},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Surfaces","u":"impact-lab.html#surfaces","x":"The Surface Response Matrix. Surfaces run down the side, kinds of contact across the top: Step, Land, Hit, Bullet, Bump, Door, Slide, Break and more when the window is wide. Each cell shows four small squares, in this order: sound, effect, mark, AI noise . Lit means this surface has it. Dim means it comes from the surface's fallback or its footstep settings. A plus means nothing answers yet. Click a cell to edit that answer under the grid. Listen plays one of its sounds. Add a pairing makes a second answer used only when the other thing is a surface you pick: a blade on wood, a metal boot on s"},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Recipes","u":"impact-lab.html#recipes","x":"Every Hit Feedback profile in the project, with when the parts of a normal, critical and killing hit start. Edit opens the profile in Hit Recipe Studio, where you drag each part along a timeline and preview it."},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Trace","u":"impact-lab.html#trace","x":"While the game plays, every contact is listed: what it was, the surface, the result, the rule that decided the surface, who caused it and how long ago. Click a row for the full story: each part (sound, effect, mark, noise) with played or the reason it was held back, in plain words: a limit, a cooldown, a player option, the feedback switch. Pause stops the list so you can read it. Only skipped hides everything that played in full. Show in Scene view draws each contact where it landed, how far its noise carried and which listeners heard it. The line under the switches shows how much of each limi"},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Test","u":"impact-lab.html#test","x":"Select an object in the scene and press a button to send that contact to it, on the side facing the Scene view. Intensity and Made by change which row answers and how strongly. The hit buttons preview a hit on the object's Hit Feedback; they never change health. In Edit Mode the buttons play a preview of the sound, effect, mark, flash and number without entering Play Mode. All feedback (or F9 while playing) switches every response off and on, to compare the game with and without. Damage still lands and AI still hears while it is off."},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Players","u":"impact-lab.html#players","x":"What players may change. Pick a policy to start from (Arcade freedom, Competitive clarity, Cinematic, Stealth readability), then set each option to Editable, Locked or Hidden, with its start value and its limits. See Player options and screens ."},{"p":"Impact Lab","h":"Settings (the gear)","u":"impact-lab.html#settings-the-gear","x":"The project-wide rules for contacts, saved in an asset in your data folder: Group What it sets General Contact responses on or off, the Surface Library that answers, the Response Graphs that run. Sound Volume, how 3D the sounds are, distance, mixer group, how many voices play at once. Limits The most sounds and effects in one burst, the burst window, the window in which near-identical contacts are combined. Kills, parries, criticals and breaks are never held back. Marks The most marks, how they fade, whether they follow moving things, the mark material. Noise for AI The base noise radius, and "},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"Surfaces and responses","u":"surfaces.html#surfaces-and-responses","x":"A Surface is an asset: Wood, Stone, Metal. It holds its footstep sounds and, as rows, its answer for every other kind of contact. A Surface Library lists your surfaces and decides which one a thing in the scene is."},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"How a thing gets its surface","u":"surfaces.html#how-a-thing-gets-its-surface","x":"The library looks, in this order, and the first match wins. The Event Trace shows which rule decided. A Surface Tag component on the object or a parent. The material, terrain layer or tile at the contact: a rule you set for it in the library, or else a keyword in its name or its texture's name (\"oak_planks\" matches Wood's keyword \"plank\"). A rule for the object's Unity tag. A keyword in the object's own name. The library's default surface. A Surface Zone (a trigger volume) overrides the ground inside it: shallow water, a rug on a stone floor."},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"A response row","u":"surfaces.html#a-response-row","x":"Each row answers one kind of contact: Hit, Projectile (bullets), Collision (bumps), Slide, Roll, Scrape, Interaction, Break, or a Custom kind with your own tag. Steps, jumps and landings use the surface's footstep sounds unless you add a row for them. Field What it does Against Only answer when the other thing is this surface. Empty answers anything. This is how a blade on wood sounds different from a club on wood. Sounds One is picked each time, never the same twice in a row. Hard layer, Hard from A second sound on top for hard contacts (debris, a ring), from the intensity you set. Min intens"},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"Fallback","u":"surfaces.html#fallback","x":"A surface can name another as its Fallback : Oak Floor falls back to Wood, a robot's body to Metal. Any kind it has no row for is answered by the fallback. In the matrix those cells are dim."},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"Weather and conditions","u":"surfaces.html#weather-and-conditions","x":"A Surface Condition is something like Wet or Snowy. While it applies it can add a sound and an effect on top of every step and contact, change volume and pitch, change how far AI hears, tint footprints, and make every surface leave footprints. A surface can also become another surface under a condition: Dirt answers as Mud when Wet, Grass as Snow when Snowy. Set that in the surface's \"When conditions\" list. SurfaceConditions.SetGlobal(wet, 1f); // it rains everywhere SurfaceConditions.ClearGlobal(); // dry again A Surface Zone can hold a condition too, for a puddle or a snowed-in corner."},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"Footwear","u":"surfaces.html#footwear","x":"A Footwear asset changes how a character's steps sound: volume, pitch, how far AI hears, and an extra sound per step (armour clinking). Set it on Surface Footsteps ( steps.Footwear = heavyArmour ). If it names what the sole is made of, step rows set Against that surface play on top."},{"p":"Surfaces and responses","h":"What ships","u":"surfaces.html#what-ships","x":"The sample library has Wood, Stone, Metal, Glass, Ice, Grass, Leaves, Carpet, Dirt, Gravel, Sand, Snow, Mud, Water and Flesh, each with rows for hits, bullets and bumps, most with slide and roll loops and breaks where they make sense, plus a Blade surface to use as a weapon material. Wet and Snowy conditions and four footwear presets are in the Samples folder. Sounds from Kenney (CC0) and the others listed in Third-Party Notices."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"Contacts","u":"contacts.html#contacts","x":"A contact is one thing touching another: a step, a hit, a bullet, a bump. Responsive FX turns every contact into the same small piece of data, finds the surface it landed on, and plays that surface's answer. You rarely need code."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"With no code: Physics Contacts","u":"contacts.html#with-no-code-physics-contacts","x":"Add Physics Contacts to anything with a Rigidbody (3D or 2D). Bumps: every collision faster than Min Speed plays the touched surface's Collision row, as hard as the impact. Slide and roll: while it keeps touching and moving, the surface's Slide or Roll loop plays and follows the speed. Break: set Break At to an intensity; a bump that hard also plays the Break row and raises On Break, where you swap the model or spawn the pieces. Made of: what this object is (Wood, Metal, Glass), so pairings answer: a metal barrel on stone."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"One line: bullets and rays","u":"contacts.html#one-line-bullets-and-rays","x":"if (Physics.Raycast(origin, direction, out var hit, range)) Contacts.PlayHit(hit); // a bullet: the Projectile row Contacts.PlayHit(hit, ContactKind.Hit, intensity: 2f); // a heavy melee blow at that point PlayHit also takes a RaycastHit2D ."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"Anything else","u":"contacts.html#anything-else","x":"Contacts.Play(ContactKind.Interaction, door, point, normal, intensity: 1.5f, source: player); Or fill in a ContactEvent yourself when you need every detail: var contact = new ContactEvent(ContactKind.Hit, point, normal) { Target = wall, Source = player, Intensity = 1.2f, SourceSurface = blade, }; Contacts.Play(in contact); Kinds: Step, Land, Jump, Hit, Projectile, Collision, Slide, Roll, Scrape, Interaction, Break, Custom (with a Tag)."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"Lasting contacts","u":"contacts.html#lasting-contacts","x":"Something dragged or rolling is one contact that lasts. Send it with a phase: ContactPhase.Begin when it starts, Update while it moves (with the current intensity), End when it stops. The loop fades in, follows the intensity and fades out. End always stops the loop, even while responses are switched off. Physics Contacts does this for you."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"What comes back","u":"contacts.html#what-comes-back","x":"Every call returns a ContactRecord : the surface found and the rule that found it, the row that answered, and for each part (sound, effect, mark, noise) whether it played or why not. Contacts.Played raises the same record for every contact, steps included."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"Limits","u":"contacts.html#limits","x":"A firefight should stay readable. The settings (Impact Lab, the gear) hold back sounds and effects past a budget per burst, combine near-identical contacts that land together, and give each object a short cooldown per row. Kills, parries, criticals and breaks are never held back. Nothing is silently dropped: the Event Trace names the limit."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"Hits and steps are contacts too","u":"contacts.html#hits-and-steps-are-contacts-too","x":"A hit on a Hit Feedback with a Body Surface also plays that surface's Hit row, so armour rings and flesh thuds. Pass the weapon's surface for pairings: new HitInfo(20f).With(blade) . A footstep plays the surface's step sounds and is recorded in the same trace."},{"p":"Contacts","h":"From other products","u":"contacts.html#from-other-products","x":"Other Rare Geometry products can send a contact with no reference to Responsive FX, through the shared Core ( SurfaceResponses.Play ). When Responsive FX is installed it answers; when it is not, nothing happens."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Noise and hearing","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#noise-and-hearing","x":"Every step and contact can make noise : a place and a radius. Noise is for your AI, not for the player's ears. It never follows the player's volume setting, so a player who turns footsteps down is still heard."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Let AI hear","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#let-ai-hear","x":"Add a Noise Listener to a guard. It hears a noise when the noise's radius reaches its ears. Hearing scales how far it hears: 2 hears twice as far. Hear steps, impacts, world choose what it listens to: footsteps and landings; hits, bullets and breaks; bumps, doors, sliding and rolling. Walls muffle makes a noise behind something solid carry less far. On Heard hands your AI the position. In code, the Heard event gives the whole noise: where, how far it carried, what kind, who made it, on which surface. listener.Heard += (NoiseListener who, in NoiseEvent noise) => agent.SetDestination(noise.Posit"},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Your own noise","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#your-own-noise","x":"GameplayNoise.Emit(new NoiseEvent { Position = transform.position, Radius = 18f, Kind = ContactKind.Custom, Source = gameObject }); GameplayNoise.Masking (0 to 1) shrinks every noise while something loud covers it: rain, machinery, a crowd. GameplayNoise.Made is raised for every noise."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Rooms and doors","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#rooms-and-doors","x":"Without rooms, sound travels in a straight line. With them, it goes through openings: Add a Sound Room to a box (or several) that covers a room. Add a Sound Portal in each doorway or window. It finds the room on each side by itself. Set the portal's Open value from your door script: 0 shut, 1 wide open. A shut door lets a little through. Noise then reaches a listener by the shortest way through open portals, losing strength at each shut one, and the sounds the player hears from another room are quieter and duller. A noise in a sealed room stays in it."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Trails","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#trails","x":"Characters leave a trail of where they stepped. A Trail Follower on an AI spots a fresh track within its Spot Radius and walks it, newest last: it raises On Track Found , then On Next Point for each point to walk to (wire it to your NavMeshAgent), and On Trail Ended when it caught up or the rest went cold. In code: Trails.FindFreshest(position, radius, out var point) and Trails.Next(point, out var next) ."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"Show the player","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#show-the-player","x":"The Noise Meter shows how much noise the player is making and changes the moment someone hears it. The Sound Ring marks where a listener who just heard the player stands. Both are on Player options and screens ."},{"p":"Noise and hearing","h":"See it","u":"noise-and-hearing.html#see-it","x":"Impact Lab's Trace tab, with Show in Scene view on, draws each noise as a ring where it landed, as far as it carried, and a line to each listener that heard it."},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"Player options and screens","u":"player-options.html#player-options-and-screens","x":"Players differ. Some want every hit to shake the screen; some get ill from it. Responsive FX gives players options, and gives you the say over which ones. One rule holds everywhere: a player option never changes damage, gameplay timing or what AI hears. It only changes what the player sees, hears and feels."},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"What players can change","u":"player-options.html#what-players-can-change","x":"Group Options Screen Camera shake, Flashes, Impact effects, Marks and footprints, Vibration, Calm repeated effects, Hit pause Numbers Damage numbers (off, critical only, all, combined), Number size, Number motion, Health bars Sound Footstep volume, Impact volume, World volume Hearing help Sound captions, Caption size, Direction cues, Sound ring, Noise meter Every option starts \"as authored\": nothing changes how your game feels until you or the player moves it."},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"You decide (Impact Lab, Players tab)","u":"player-options.html#you-decide-impact-lab-players-tab","x":"Make the Player Feedback Options asset there. For each option choose Editable (the player may change it), Locked (shown but fixed) or Hidden , and set its start value and its limits. Four policies fill the table for you: Arcade freedom, Competitive clarity, Cinematic and Stealth readability. Choices are remembered between sessions unless you turn that off and save them yourself ( PlayerFeedback.ToJson() and FromJson )."},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"The options screen","u":"player-options.html#the-options-screen","x":"Add Feedback Options Panel to any object. Call Show() from your pause menu, or give it a key. It is laid out like a console game's settings: tabs for the groups, one row per option, the value stepped with an arrow each side or dragged, a dot and a small undo beside anything that differs from your value, the highlighted option explained at the bottom, presets along the top (Default, Reduced motion, Low stimulation, Clear combat, Quiet play) and Reset all. It works with a mouse, keys or a gamepad, and on a narrow or upright screen each control moves under its name. The game's HUD is hidden while"},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"Sound for players who cannot hear it","u":"player-options.html#sound-for-players-who-cannot-hear-it","x":"Sound Captions shows a short caption for sounds that matter (\"Footsteps on gravel\"), with an arrow for where it came from. The player turns captions on (Important or All) and picks their size. captions.Show(\"Alarm\", position) adds your own. Sound Ring shows sounds as marks on a ring around the middle of the screen: a glowing arc on the side each sound came from, with an icon for footsteps, an impact or something loud, and an eye for a listener who just heard the player. Marks turn with the camera. ring.Show(position, SoundRing.Cue.Loud) adds your own. It can show always (stealth games, shooter"},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"The player's side of a hit","u":"player-options.html#the-players-side-of-a-hit","x":"Player Hit Feedback on the player shows a damage vignette, a glowing arc on the side each hit came from (it keeps pointing at the attacker while the camera turns; a single arrow is the other style), a heartbeat pulse at low health, and rumble on a gamepad or phone."},{"p":"Player options and screens","h":"Works with your settings menu","u":"player-options.html#works-with-your-settings-menu","x":"If another product provides accessibility settings through the shared Core (reduced motion, shake, flash and haptics scales), Responsive FX follows them. When nothing else does, the player's choices here are offered the same way, so other products can follow them."},{"p":"Response Graph","h":"Response Graph","u":"response-graph.html#response-graph","x":"Surface rows cover almost everything. The Response Graph is for the special cases a row cannot say: \"a critical hit on metal also rings a bell and is twice as loud to guards\", \"a bullet into anything wet adds a splash\"."},{"p":"Response Graph","h":"Make one","u":"response-graph.html#make-one","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Response Graph , then New graph . Add boxes with Add a box , drag a dot onto another box to link them, drag a box by its title to move it. Add the graph to the Response Settings (Impact Lab, the gear, Response Graphs). A graph that is not listed there does not run. A graph runs once for each contact, from its When boxes."},{"p":"Response Graph","h":"The boxes","u":"response-graph.html#the-boxes","x":"Starts When contact: runs for the kinds you tick (Hit, Projectile, Collision and so on). Questions (two ways out, yes and no) Outcome is: critical, kill, blocked, parried, guard broken, staggered, armoured, immune, missed, shielded, healed. Surface is: the surface the contact landed on. Made of is: what did the touching (a Blade, a Boot). Intensity at least: how hard it was. Tag is: the contact's own tag, for Custom kinds. Condition at least: how wet, how snowy, where it landed. Chance: yes this often. Target has tag, Source has tag: Unity tags. Actions (one way out, to what happens next) Play"},{"p":"Response Graph","h":"Good to know","u":"response-graph.html#good-to-know","x":"A graph runs after the surface is found, for single contacts (not for the updates of a lasting slide or roll). A graph cannot loop for ever: it stops after a fixed number of boxes. The Event Trace shows how many graph actions ran for each contact. The Samples folder has a small worked example (critical on metal; a bullet in the wet)."},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Integrations","u":"integrations.html#integrations","x":"Responsive FX needs no package. Each extra below switches itself on when its package is in the project, and compiles to nothing when it is not. With You get Input System Gamepad rumble on the player's hits. Without it, phone vibration still works on Android and iOS. Cinemachine Camera shake as an impulse, so it never fights your virtual cameras. TextMesh Pro Damage numbers drawn with your TextMesh Pro fonts. URP A post-processing pulse on strong hits (darker edges, a colour split), set in the profile's Pulse section. Needs post-processing on the camera. HDRP The same pulse for HDRP. Netcode fo"},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Feel (More Mountains)","u":"integrations.html#feel-more-mountains","x":"When Feel is in the project, turn the bridge on in Impact Lab (the gear, Bridges). You get: two Feel feedbacks under Responsive FX in a Feel player's Add Feedback list: Contact (plays a surface's answer where the player is played) and Hit (plays a hit on a Hit Feedback); two components that go the other way: Feel On Contact and Feel On Hit play a Feel player whenever a contact or a hit happens, at its position and with its intensity. Removing Feel turns the bridge off by itself, so the project keeps compiling."},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Your own sound engine (FMOD, Wwise or anything else)","u":"integrations.html#your-own-sound-engine-fmod-wwise-or-anything-else","x":"Sounds can be handed to your code instead of Unity's AudioSource: Contacts.SoundPlayer = myPlayer; // implements IContactSoundPlayer Your player receives the contact, the surface, the row, the clip that was picked, and the volume and pitch. Effects, marks and AI noise still play as usual. Footsteps have the same hook on Surface Footsteps (see Steps, Extending)."},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Visual scripting and other tools","u":"integrations.html#visual-scripting-and-other-tools","x":"Everything a designer needs is a UnityEvent or a public method with simple arguments: Hit Feedback's HitAmount , CriticalHit , Parry , GuardBreak ; Physics Contacts' On Break; Noise Listener's On Heard; Trail Follower's events; the options panel's Show and Hide . Any tool that can call a method or listen to a UnityEvent can drive them."},{"p":"Integrations","h":"Other Rare Geometry products","u":"integrations.html#other-rare-geometry-products","x":"Nothing depends on anything. When another product is installed, the two find each other through the shared Core: its floors and pieces carry the shared Surface Tag , so steps and hits on them answer here with no setup; it can send a contact (a door slamming, a piece placed) and Responsive FX answers it; accessibility settings (reduced motion, shake, flash, haptics) are shared both ways."},{"p":"Hit feedback","h":"Hit feedback","u":"hits.html#hit-feedback","x":"Make every hit land. Add one Hit Feedback component to anything that can be hit, and every hit plays the whole reaction together: a damage number, a hit flash, hitstop, camera shake, knockback, squash, a health bar, a sound, sparks and, on the killing blow, a kill moment. Pick a genre preset for the right feel in one click, and when something doesn't play, the Why No Feedback? window tells you why in plain words, with a one-click fix."},{"p":"Hit feedback","h":"What it does","u":"hits.html#what-it-does","x":"Part What it gives you Hit Feedback The one component. Hit it with one line of code, a UnityEvent, or the optional Health component. Profiles and presets Arcade, Action RPG, Souls-like, Cartoon, Shooter, Idle and Mobile, Minimal, plus one Juice slider. Hit Recipe Studio Design a hit as a moment: drag when the number, flash, shake, sound and sparks start for normal, critical and killing hits, and preview it. Damage numbers Kinds (normal, critical, heal, shield, miss, block, damage over time, status), four styles with critical bursts, combining, five motions, 1.2K formats, icons, Display as. Poo"},{"p":"Hit feedback","h":"Where to start","u":"hits.html#where-to-start","x":"Quick start : hit feedback on your own enemy in five minutes. Demo scenes : an arena fight, a gallery of every preset, a 2D level. Why No Feedback? : when something doesn't play."},{"p":"Hit feedback","h":"Compatibility","u":"hits.html#compatibility","x":"Unity 6000.0.85f1 and newer Unity 6 versions. Built-in, URP and HDRP. 2D and 3D. Both input systems (Responsive FX itself reads no input; rumble uses the Input System package when it is installed). No package dependencies."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"hits-quick-start.html#quick-start","x":"Hit feedback on your own enemy in about five minutes."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Add Hit Feedback","u":"hits-quick-start.html#1-add-hit-feedback","x":"Select your enemy (in the Hierarchy, or a prefab in the Project window) and open Tools > Responsive FX > Setup Wizard . Pick a Preset ( Existing Game (safe) if your game already has combat) and press Add Hit Feedback . Prefabs get a new variant in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX/Prefabs ; your original prefab is never changed. You can also just add the component: Add Component > Responsive FX > Hit Feedback . It works at once with the Action RPG preset."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Hit it","u":"hits-quick-start.html#2-hit-it","x":"Pick whichever fits your game: One line of code , from your weapon, projectile or combat script: using RareGeometry.ResponsiveFX; HitFeedback.Hit(enemy, 25); // a normal hit of 25 HitFeedback.Hit(enemy, new HitInfo(60, HitKind.Critical).At(point)); // a critical hit where it landed Your own health script , no code: the Setup Wizard finds it and adds a Hit Feedback Bridge that watches its health value. See Hook it into your game . Responsive FX's Health component (Setup Wizard: Add Responsive FX's basic Health). Call health.Damage(25) and the feedback plays by itself, including the kill moment "},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Press Play","u":"hits-quick-start.html#3-press-play","x":"Hit it. You see the number, the flash, the freeze, the shake and the push. Use the buttons in the Hit Feedback inspector ( Hit , Critical , Heal , Kill ) to try it without any code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Make it feel like your game","u":"hits-quick-start.html#4-make-it-feel-like-your-game","x":"Change the Preset for a different feel, or press Save As Profile to change every value. Drag the Juice slider for more or less of everything. Add Player Hit Feedback to your player for a damage vignette, direction arrows and rumble. Nothing happens? Open Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Feedback? . It lists every part of each hit, what played and why anything was skipped, with a fix."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Setup Wizard","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#setup-wizard","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Setup Wizard adds Hit Feedback to your own enemies, players and props, one or a whole folder at once. It also opens from the right-click menu in the Hierarchy and the Project window ( Responsive FX > Add Hit Feedback (Setup Wizard) )."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"1. What to set up","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#1-what-to-set-up","x":"Select objects and press Use Selection . You can pick: prefabs in the Project window, a folder : every prefab in it, objects in the scene. For each one the wizard shows what it found: the renderers it will flash (meshes, skinned meshes, sprites), what will be pushed (Rigidbody, Rigidbody 2D, Character Controller, NavMesh Agent or the transform), whether an Animator will pause in hitstop, and whether it already has health. Problems are listed under it, for example \"Marked Static\" (static batched objects can't flash) or \"Has missing scripts\" (reimport it first)."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"2. The feel","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#2-the-feel","x":"Pick a Preset , or drag in a profile you already made. You can change everything later. Adding to a game that already has combat? Pick Existing Game (safe) : numbers, flash, bar, sound and sparks, and nothing that moves, freezes or scales your objects or touches your camera."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"3. Who controls health?","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#3-who-controls-health","x":"Find it for me (recommended): looks for your own health script by name (Health, HP, Damageable, Stats...). When it finds one it adds a Hit Feedback Bridge that watches its health value, so your game stays in charge. When there is none it adds Responsive FX's Health. It never adds a second health system next to yours. My game already has health : always adds a Bridge (watching your health script when one is found; otherwise connect your events to it, see Hook it into your game ). Add Responsive FX's basic Health with a Max health : Responsive FX's Health component, which plays feedback by itsel"},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Extras","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#extras","x":"This is the player : adds Player Hit Feedback (vignette, direction arrows, low-health pulse, rumble)."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"4. Add it","u":"hits-setup-wizard.html#4-add-it","x":"Add Hit Feedback : Prefabs get a new Prefab Variant called \"Name (Hit Feedback)\" in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX/Prefabs . Your original prefab is not changed (a test checks its file stays byte-identical) and still receives your edits and pack updates. Use the variant in your scenes. Scene objects get the component directly. Undo works. Prefabs with missing scripts are skipped; reimport them and try again."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","u":"hit-feedback.html#hit-feedback-and-hitting-things","x":"Hit Feedback is the one component Responsive FX needs. Put it on anything that can be hit: an enemy, the player, a crate. Every hit then plays the whole reaction from its profile."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Hitting it","u":"hit-feedback.html#hitting-it","x":"Way How One line of code HitFeedback.Hit(target, 25); finds the Hit Feedback on the target or its nearest parent, so passing the collider you hit works. Returns false when there is none (the debugger remembers it). A full hit HitFeedback.Hit(target, new HitInfo(25, HitKind.Critical).At(point).By(attacker)); Responsive FX's Health health.Damage(25) , health.Damage(hitInfo) , health.Heal(10) , health.Kill() . Feedback plays by itself. UnityEvents and animation events Methods with one float: HitAmount , CriticalHit , HealAmount , ShieldHit , DamageOverTime , KillingHit . And Miss , Block , ShowSt"},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"HitInfo: one hit","u":"hit-feedback.html#hitinfo-one-hit","x":"Field What it does Amount The number shown. Kind Normal, Critical, Heal, Shield, Miss, Block, DamageOverTime or Status. Each kind has its own look and strength. IsKill ( .Killing() ) The killing blow: plays the kill moment. Point ( .At(point) ) Where it landed: numbers (Hit Point anchor) and particles start there. Direction ( .From(dir) ) or Source ( .By(attacker) ) Which way it travelled: knockback, camera punch and the player's damage arrow use it. Without either, Responsive FX pushes away from the camera. Text A word instead of the number (\"Stunned\"). Icon An icon beside the number. Strengt"},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"What each kind plays","u":"hit-feedback.html#what-each-kind-plays","x":"Kind Plays Normal, Critical, Shield, Block Everything (criticals stronger, blocks softer). Heal Number (green, with a +), flash, squash, bar, sound. No push, shake or freeze. Damage over time Small combining numbers, a light flash, bar, sound. No freeze, shake or push. Miss, Status The word and its sound only."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"The component","u":"hit-feedback.html#the-component","x":"Setting What it does Profile / Preset The feel. Without a profile asset the preset's shared profile is used; Save As Profile makes an asset you can change. Juice (with a profile asset) More or less of everything. Renderers to flash Empty: every mesh, skinned mesh and sprite under the object (lower LOD levels and children with their own Hit Feedback are skipped). Visual (squash) What squashes and pops. Use a child when the root also moves or scales. Number and bar anchor Where numbers and the bar appear. Empty: above the renderers. Health source Your own health script (anything with IHealthSour"},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Preview in the inspector","u":"hit-feedback.html#preview-in-the-inspector","x":"In Play Mode the Hit , Critical , Heal and Kill buttons play on the selected objects, and the inspector lists what the last hit played and why anything was skipped."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Profiles, presets and Juice","u":"hits-profiles.html#profiles-presets-and-juice","x":"A Hit Feedback Profile holds everything a hit plays. Share one between all your goblins, another for bosses."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Make one","u":"hits-profiles.html#make-one","x":"Assets > Create > Responsive FX > Profile from Preset > (preset) : a new profile already set to that feel. Or press Save As Profile on a Hit Feedback that uses a preset (saved in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX/Profiles )."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"The presets","u":"hits-profiles.html#the-presets","x":"Preset Feel Arcade Big, bright and bouncy, with strong shake and hitstop. Kill pop and a screen flash. Action RPG Clear numbers that add up, crits that pop, a bar on hit. Souls-like Heavy and grounded: a global hitstop, rim flash, slow drain, quiet numbers at the hit point. Cartoon Squash and stretch, bouncy numbers ending in \"!\", bright colours, sparks and puffs. Shooter Small fast numbers at the hit point, a rim flash, light shake, no freeze. Idle and Mobile Many numbers that add up, short forms (1.2K), bigger numbers for bigger amounts, no shake. Minimal A clean flash and a small number, no"},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Juice","u":"hits-profiles.html#juice","x":"One slider for everything. 0 keeps only numbers, bars and sounds; 1 is the preset; 2 doubles flash, shake, hitstop, knockback, squash and particles. There is also a master Juice for the whole game in HitFeedbackSettings.Juice , for an options menu."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Scale with amount","u":"hits-profiles.html#scale-with-amount","x":"Turn on Scale with amount and bigger hits feel bigger: shake, hitstop, knockback and squash scale with the amount compared with Full strength amount . (Number size by amount is a separate setting under Numbers.)"},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Kinds","u":"hits-profiles.html#kinds","x":"Each kind (Normal, Critical, Heal, Shield, Miss, Block, Damage Over Time, Status) has a colour, a colour-blind safe colour, a word, a suffix, a size, a strength, an icon and its sounds."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"The sections","u":"hits-profiles.html#the-sections","x":"Numbers, Flash, Hitstop, Camera shake and punch, Knockback, Squash, Floating health bar, Sound, Particles and Kill moment. Each is on its own page here. The inspector shows a live preview of the numbers as you change them."},{"p":"Hit Recipe Studio","h":"Hit Recipe Studio","u":"hit-recipe-studio.html#hit-recipe-studio","x":"A hit is a moment, not a list of effects. Hit Recipe Studio shows one hit reaction as a timeline: you drag each part to when it should start, press Preview, and every hit in your game plays it the same way. Open it from Tools > Responsive FX > Hit Recipe Studio , from the Hit Recipe Studio button on a Hit Feedback, or from Open in Hit Recipe Studio on a profile."},{"p":"Hit Recipe Studio","h":"The window","u":"hit-recipe-studio.html#the-window","x":"Profile and Preview target : the profile you are designing, and the object that plays the preview. Selecting an object with Hit Feedback in the scene sets both. A target that plays a preset gets a Make a profile button first (presets are shared and can't be changed). Normal, Critical, Kill : the three reactions. Kill is used by the killing hit, Critical by critical hits, Normal by every other hit (normal, heal, shield, block, miss, damage over time, status). The timeline (the first second after the hit): Hitstop always lands on the hit itself; its length comes from the profile's Hitstop settin"},{"p":"Hit Recipe Studio","h":"Ideas that work","u":"hit-recipe-studio.html#ideas-that-work","x":"Impact first: flash, sound and sparks on the hit, the shake 20 ms later, the number at 60 ms. The number seems to come out of the impact. Heavy blow: the same, with the shake at 50 ms and the number at 120 ms. Reads as weight. Final blow: for kills, the big number last (about 180 ms), after the burst. Keep the flash at 0 for most hits: players read the flash as \"it connected\"."},{"p":"Hit Recipe Studio","h":"How it behaves in your game","u":"hit-recipe-studio.html#how-it-behaves-in-your-game","x":"Delays run on real time, so a hitstop or slow motion never holds them back. A delayed part plays with the target's settings at that moment (if its renderers changed, it uses the new ones). Disabling, pooling or destroying the target drops the parts still waiting, so nothing pops up on a reused enemy. On a killing hit, the kill action (disable or destroy) waits until the last part has started. No garbage: waiting parts sit in a small fixed list on each target (24 at most; past that a part plays at once). All zero (the default for a new profile or a preset) plays everything on the hit, exactly a"},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Damage numbers","u":"damage-numbers.html#damage-numbers","x":"Numbers and words drawn on top of the finished frame (after post effects, so bloom never smears them), the same in every render pipeline. They are pooled and steady play makes no garbage."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Number styles","u":"damage-numbers.html#number-styles","x":"Four finished looks, one click each (the profile inspector's Number style buttons, or NumberStyles.Apply in code). Your font, sizes and colours per kind stay. Style Look Criticals Classic White with a light shine and a dark outline. Pop and shake. Arcade Punch Hot fill fading to orange, thick plum outline, a punchy pop. Rays burst out behind the number; it leans. Fantasy Relic Ivory into antique gold, charcoal outline, a gentle arc. A gold halo grows and fades. Tactical Clean Crisp near-white, navy outline, a short slide with no bounce. Cyan corner brackets snap outwards. The presets pick a st"},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Settings (Profile > Numbers)","u":"damage-numbers.html#settings-profile--numbers","x":"Setting What it does Display as Number, Number And Icon, Icon, Word (the kind's word), None. Also per object under Overrides. Motion Rise, Arc (pops up and falls to a side), Punch (pops big and settles), Slide, Stay. Space Screen: always on top, the same size at any distance. World: sized by distance, and can hide behind walls. Anchor Above the target (above its bar), at the hit point, or at its middle. Plus an offset. Size Text height in pixels at 1080p; scales with the screen, each kind's size and the player's text size. Font, Bold Any font asset in your project; empty uses Unity's built-in "},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Per kind (Profile > Kinds)","u":"damage-numbers.html#per-kind-profile--kinds","x":"Colour, colour-blind safe colour, word (\"Miss\", \"Blocked\"), suffix (\"!\" makes 123!), size, icon."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Numbers anywhere","u":"damage-numbers.html#numbers-anywhere","x":"DamageNumbers.Show(position, 25, HitKind.Heal); // a number, Action RPG style or a profile you pass DamageNumbers.ShowText(position, \"Level up!\", HitKind.Status, myProfile); DamageNumbers.Hidden = true; // hide them (for a cut scene)"},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Your TextMesh Pro font","u":"damage-numbers.html#your-textmesh-pro-font","x":"With TextMesh Pro in your project (it comes with Unity 6's UI package), add TextMesh Pro Numbers (Add Component > Responsive FX) to any object in the scene and drag in your TMP font asset and, if you like, a material preset (outline, glow, gradient). While it is enabled every number is shown with TextMesh Pro in your font, in world space, facing the camera; kinds, colours, words, formatting and sizes still come from your profiles. Turn it off and the Responsive FX draws its own numbers again. In 1.0 it shows one number per hit (it doesn't add hits into one growing number)."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Your own text (UI Toolkit, your prefab)","u":"damage-numbers.html#your-own-text-ui-toolkit-your-prefab","x":"Set DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay to your own object implementing IDamageNumberDisplay . Responsive FX hands it every number with its text already worked out (\"1.2K\", \"+25\", \"Miss\"), its colour, size and icon. See Code ."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"When numbers are hidden","u":"damage-numbers.html#when-numbers-are-hidden","x":"The screen layer hides while the game is paused (Time.timeScale at 0 and not set by Responsive FX's own hitstop) and when HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible is false, for example while your own menu is open. Numbers from a scene are cleared when a new scene loads on its own."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Hit flash","u":"hit-flash.html#hit-flash","x":"The target flashes a colour for a moment. It works on meshes, skinned meshes and sprites, with any material and any shader, in Built-in, URP and HDRP."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"How it works (and why your materials are safe)","u":"hit-flash.html#how-it-works-and-why-your-materials-are-safe","x":"On the first hit Responsive FX makes a copy of each renderer (sharing its mesh, bones or sprite) drawn with Responsive FX's own flash shader just in front of the original. The copy shows only while flashing. Your materials, shaders and material slots are never changed, and a mesh with several materials flashes all of them. Skipped, with the reason shown in Why No Feedback?: static batched objects (their mesh is merged into one), particle, trail and line renderers, and lower LOD levels (the first LOD is flashed). If you add renderers at runtime, call RebuildFlash() ."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Settings (Profile > Flash)","u":"hit-flash.html#settings-profile--flash","x":"Setting What it does Mode Fill (the whole object), Rim (only the edges glow; good on large or detailed models), Fill And Rim. Color The flash colour for normal hits. Use kind colors Gold criticals, blue shield hits and so on. Heals always flash their own green. Duration, Strength How long (real time, so it shows during hitstop) and how strong. Blinks Blink this many times instead of one flash: the classic invulnerability blink. Rim width Higher is a thinner edge. Per object: Overrides > Flash Color."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Flash safety","u":"hit-flash.html#flash-safety","x":"With flash safety on (the default), an object flashes at full strength at most three times a second; extra flashes in that second are dimmed. See Accessibility ."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"HDRP","u":"hit-flash.html#hdrp","x":"The flash is drawn in HDRP's unlit pass, before tone mapping, so it follows your exposure like everything else in the frame."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","u":"hits-motion.html#hitstop-shake-knockback-and-squash","x":"Every one of these puts things back exactly afterwards: the object's velocity, the game's time scale, your camera, the object's scale."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Hitstop (Profile > Hitstop)","u":"hits-motion.html#hitstop-profile--hitstop","x":"Setting What it does Mode Target Only: just the hit object pauses (its Animators, Rigidbodies, Rigidbody 2Ds, particle systems, NavMesh Agent and your own scripts with IHitstopReceiver). Global: the whole game freezes for a moment. Off. Duration Real seconds for a normal hit (0.05 is about three frames). Each kind's strength scales it. Max duration The longest a single stop may last. A second hit while frozen extends the freeze up to this, never stacks past it. Freeze attacker Target Only: the attacker (the hit's Source, when it has Hit Feedback) freezes for the same moment. Global time scale "},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Camera shake and punch (Profile > Camera shake and punch)","u":"hits-motion.html#camera-shake-and-punch-profile--camera-shake-and-punch","x":"Trauma based: each hit adds trauma, the shake is trauma squared (small hits stay subtle, big ones build up) and it fades smoothly. Setting What it does Trauma How much a normal hit adds (0 to 1). Max offset, Max angle, Frequency, Recovery The feel of the shake. Punch A quick nudge in the hit's direction. FOV kick A quick zoom (on 2D orthographic cameras, the same as a size change). Max distance Hits farther from the camera shake less, down to nothing. Use Cinemachine Cinemachine 3 impulses instead (your camera needs a Cinemachine Impulse Listener). It never fights your camera controller. The s"},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Knockback (Profile > Knockback)","u":"hits-motion.html#knockback-profile--knockback","x":"Mover What happens Rigidbody A push (velocity change, or an impulse when Ignore Mass is off). Rigidbody 2D A push along the hit, with an upward part. Character Controller A short glide using Move, so it respects collisions. NavMesh Agent A short glide using the agent's Move. Nothing (transform) A short glide of the transform, when Move Transform is on. Force, Upward, Duration (for glides), Ignore Mass, Move Transform. The direction comes from the hit's Direction, its Source, its Point, or else away from the camera."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Squash (Profile > Squash)","u":"hits-motion.html#squash-profile--squash","x":"Scale Punch, Squash And Stretch, or Wobble, by an amount and for a duration. It works on the Visual you set (a child, so the root's own scale and collider stay as they are) and always ends at the exact starting scale. If your animation also scales the object, Responsive FX squashes on top of it."},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Health and health bars","u":"health-bars.html#health-and-health-bars","x":""},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Where health comes from","u":"health-bars.html#where-health-comes-from","x":"Bars show the health of the first of these the object has: Your own health script with IHealthSource (four properties: CurrentHealth, MaxHealth, CurrentShield, MaxShield). Drag it into Hit Feedback > Health source, or just put it on the same object. Responsive FX's Health component (optional, basic). Values you set with hitFeedback.SetHealth(current, max, shield, maxShield) ."},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"The Health component","u":"health-bars.html#the-health-component","x":"Max health, start health, an optional shield that takes damage first, an invulnerable time after each hit, and events (On Damaged, On Healed, On Died). Every change plays feedback on the object's Hit Feedback: a damage number, a \"Shield\" hit when the shield takes it all, a green heal, and the kill moment when health reaches zero. health.Damage(25); health.Damage(new HitInfo(40, HitKind.Critical).At(point).By(attacker)); health.Heal(10); health.AddShield(20); health.Kill(); health.Revive(); // full health again; a kill pop is undone"},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Floating bar (Profile > Floating health bar)","u":"health-bars.html#floating-bar-profile--floating-health-bar","x":"Setting What it does Visibility On Hit (then fades), Always, While Damaged, Never. Show time, Fade time How long it stays after a hit and how it fades. Width, Height, Offset Pixels at 1080p, and metres above the target. Numbers start above the bar, so they never overlap it. Fill, Low, Drain, Back, Shield, Border colours The look. Below Low Health the fill turns the low colour. Drain delay, Drain speed The lost part waits, then drains away. Segment size A tick every this much health. Rounded Round ends. Per object: Overrides > Health Bar (off for a boss that has a boss bar). For one character's"},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Boss bar and player HUD bar: Screen Health Bar","u":"health-bars.html#boss-bar-and-player-hud-bar-screen-health-bar","x":"Add Screen Health Bar to the boss or the player: Boss : wide, at the top centre, with a name. Turns on with the boss. Player HUD : compact, bottom left, with \"350 / 500\" inside. Custom : your own anchor (0,0 bottom left to 1,1 top right), offset and size. It has its own bar settings (colours, delayed drain, segments, shield), shows the numbers, can hide while full, and hides when the target is defeated."},{"p":"Player feedback","h":"Player feedback","u":"player-feedback.html#player-feedback","x":"Add Player Hit Feedback to your player (next to its Hit Feedback or Health). It reacts to the player's hits by itself; or call PlayerHit(hitInfo) from your own code. Part What it does Settings Damage vignette The screen edges flash a colour; bigger hits come closer to the full strength. Colour, strength, time Damage direction An arrow around the screen centre points at where the hit came from (its Source, Direction or Point). Colour, time, radius, size Low health The screen edges pulse while health is below a threshold (a steady glow with Reduce Motion). Threshold, colour, rate, strength Rumbl"},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#sound-particles-and-the-kill-moment","x":""},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Sound (Profile > Sound, clips under Kinds)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#sound-profile--sound-clips-under-kinds","x":"Each kind has its own clips; one is picked at random. Kinds without clips use the Normal kind's (except Miss and Status). Setting What it does Volume, Pitch min and max Random pitch for variety. Combo pitch step Hits that combine into one number raise the pitch a little each time. Cooldown The shortest time between two sounds from the same target, so rapid hits don't stack. Spatial blend 0: the same everywhere. 1: from the target's position. Output An Audio Mixer group. Sounds play from a pool of 16 voices shared by every target; the master volume is HitFeedbackSettings.Volume ."},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Particles (Profile > Particles)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#particles-profile--particles","x":"Responsive FX's own Sparks (bright, stretched, falling), a Puff (soft, rising), both, or none. Count, size, speed, colour (or each kind's colour). They play where the hit landed and fly back along the hit. Add your own prefab (any particle effect) and it is spawned at the hit and returned to a pool after its lifetime."},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Kill moment (Profile > Kill moment)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#kill-moment-profile--kill-moment","x":"On the killing blow ( HitInfo.IsKill , KillingHit , or Health reaching zero): Setting What it does Slow motion, Slow motion time The game slows for a moment (safe: see Hitstop). Number size The final number is bigger. Extra shake, Extra hitstop A harder last hit. Screen flash, colour A quick full-screen flash (dimmed by flash safety when kills come fast). Pop and fade, Pop time The target pops up and shrinks away. After, After delay Nothing (your code takes over), Disable or Destroy the object. Pooling enemies? A popped object comes back at its own size when you enable it again or call health."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Accessibility","u":"accessibility.html#accessibility","x":"Built in, and meant for your game's options menu. Set the starting values in Project Settings > Responsive FX (saved in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX/Resources ), and change them while playing through HitFeedbackSettings : Setting What it does ReduceMotion No camera shake, punch or zoom kick, no global hitstop or slow motion, no knockback or squash. Numbers fade in place without moving or shaking. The low-health pulse glows steadily. FlashSafety , MaxFlashesPerSecond An object flashes at full strength at most 3 times a second (common photosensitivity guidance); extra flashes are dimmed."},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D games","u":"hits-2d.html#2d-games","x":"Everything works in 2D the same way. Responsive FX notices a 2D target (a Rigidbody 2D, or sprites and no 3D Rigidbody) and: flashes the sprite's own shape (sorted just above it, following its sprite animation, flip and sorting layer), pushes along the hit in the X and Y plane with an upward part, using the Rigidbody 2D (or the transform), shakes and kicks an orthographic camera (the zoom kick changes its size), shows numbers and bars as in 3D (Screen space keeps them the same size on screen), points the player's damage arrow by screen up and right. Tip: put Hit Feedback on the root and set Vi"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Make it yours","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything a player sees, hears or feels can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Start from a preset","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#start-from-a-preset","x":"Eight presets change every part at once (see Profiles ). Then the Juice slider for more or less of everything. The demos' Make it yours panel (M) switches presets and the main choices while you play."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Every part can be switched off","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#every-part-can-be-switched-off","x":"In the profile each part has its own switch (Numbers, Flash, Hitstop mode, Shake, Knockback, Squash, Bar visibility, Sound, Particles, Kill moment). Per object, Overrides turns any part on or off for that object only, and gives it its own flash colour, number colour and size, and floating bar style (a boss with a wide gold bar and big gold numbers, minions with the default)."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"The look","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#the-look","x":"What Where Number style in one click: Classic, Arcade Punch, Fantasy Relic, Tactical Clean Profile > Number style buttons Numbers: Display as, motion, size, font, bold, outline, shadow, shine, gradient, critical burst and lean, format Profile > Numbers When each part of a hit starts (normal, critical, kill) Hit Recipe Studio Each kind's colour, colour-blind colour, word, suffix, size, icon Profile > Kinds Where numbers appear: above the target, at the hit point, the middle, an offset, your own anchor Profile > Numbers, Hit Feedback > Number and bar anchor Flash mode, colour, blinks Profile > F"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Your own text and UI","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#your-own-text-and-ui","x":"Your own font : drag any font asset into Profile > Numbers > Font. Your TextMesh Pro font : add TextMesh Pro Numbers and drag in your TMP font asset. Your own number display (UI Toolkit, your prefab): DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay . Your own bars and HUD : read health from IHealthSource , react to HitFeedback.AnyHit , OnHit , OnKilled , and set Responsive FX's bars to Never. Your own words in any language: HitFeedbackSettings.Translate ."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Left as they are on purpose","u":"hits-make-it-yours.html#left-as-they-are-on-purpose","x":"The Why No Feedback? window's look (an editor tool, not seen by players) and the demo panels (examples only)."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hook it into your game","u":"hits-integration.html#hook-it-into-your-game","x":"Your game already decides who gets hurt, how much and when they die. Responsive FX only shows it. This page is about connecting the two without changing how your game works."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Pick one route","u":"hits-integration.html#pick-one-route","x":"Your game has... Use Code? A health script with a health number (a field or property) Hit Feedback Bridge, Watch None A health script with UnityEvents (OnDamaged, OnHealthChanged, OnDied) Hit Feedback Bridge, events None A place in code where damage is dealt HitFeedback.Hit(target, amount) One line No health at all yet Responsive FX's Health component None Projectiles, hitboxes or traps but no damage code The Hit On Contact recipe None Use one route per enemy. Two routes (for example a Bridge watching your health and a call to HitFeedback.Hit in your damage code) show every hit twice. The Setu"},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"The Existing Game preset","u":"hits-integration.html#the-existing-game-preset","x":"Start with Existing Game (safe) when your game already has combat. It shows numbers, the flash, the bar, sound and sparks, and nothing else: no knockback, no squash, no hitstop, no camera shake, no slow motion, and the kill moment never hides or destroys your object. Your movement, physics, camera and time are never touched. Once that works, turn on the extras you want one at a time in the profile."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hit Feedback Bridge","u":"hits-integration.html#hit-feedback-bridge","x":"Add Hit Feedback Bridge next to Hit Feedback (Add Component > Responsive FX). Watch a value (no code). Press Find my health script , or drag your health script into Health script and pick its health value from the list (any float, int or double field or property). Optionally pick its max health value for the bar. Then every drop plays a hit, reaching Dead at or below plays the kill, a rise shows healing, and the bar follows the value. Coming back from zero (a respawn) shows nothing and tidies the object up. The Bridge only reads: it never changes your health. Reading it every frame makes no ga"},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Recipes","u":"hits-integration.html#recipes","x":""},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your own health script, in code","u":"hits-integration.html#your-own-health-script-in-code","x":"public void TakeDamage(float amount, Vector3 point, Transform attacker) { hp -= amount; HitFeedback.Hit(gameObject, new HitInfo(amount).At(point).By(attacker).Killing(hp <= 0)); } Add IHealthSource to the same script (four properties, see Code, events and your own UI ) and the bars follow your health too."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Projectiles, 3D and 2D","u":"hits-integration.html#projectiles-3d-and-2d","x":"Put Hit On Contact (Recipes) on the bullet. It works with collisions and triggers in 3D and 2D, uses the exact contact point and the bullet's direction, and can destroy the bullet on its first hit. Turn on Feedback only when your own script deals the damage. From your own projectile code: void OnCollisionEnter(Collision c) { HitFeedback.Hit(c.collider, new HitInfo(damage).At(c.GetContact(0).point).From(-c.relativeVelocity)); } HitFeedback.Hit takes the collider you touched: it finds the Hit Feedback on it or on a parent, so hitting an arm collider on a character works."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Melee hitboxes and trigger zones","u":"hits-integration.html#melee-hitboxes-and-trigger-zones","x":"Hit On Contact on the hitbox. Each target is hit once per Once every seconds, so a hitbox that stays inside an enemy for several frames, or touches three of its colliders, gives one hit."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hitscan weapons","u":"hits-integration.html#hitscan-weapons","x":"Call Hitscan Hit 's Fire() (or FireShot from a UnityEvent). It casts a ray forward and plays the hit at the point struck. Or in your own code: HitFeedback.Hit(rayHit.collider, new HitInfo(damage).At(rayHit.point).From(ray.direction)) ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Visual Scripting","u":"hits-integration.html#visual-scripting","x":"Every Bridge method and HitFeedback.HitAmount , CriticalHit , HealAmount , KillingHit take one number, so they show up as plain nodes. In a Script Graph, get the Hit Feedback (or Bridge) component and call the method with your damage."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Pooled enemies","u":"hits-integration.html#pooled-enemies","x":"Nothing to do: Hit Feedback puts the object back (colour, scale, frozen state) when it is disabled and enabled again. If you reuse an object without disabling it, call hitFeedback.ResetVisual() when you respawn it."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Ragdolls","u":"hits-integration.html#ragdolls","x":"The Setup Wizard spots ragdolls (three or more jointed bones) and turns off knockback and squash for them, because physics owns the body. The flash, numbers, bar, sound and sparks still play. Put Hit Feedback on the root; hits on any bone find it."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Many renderers, LODs and custom shaders","u":"hits-integration.html#many-renderers-lods-and-custom-shaders","x":"The flash draws a copy of each renderer with Responsive FX's own shader, so it shows whatever your material is, custom shaders included, and never changes your materials. The copy is solid: it doesn't follow alpha cutouts, dissolves or vertex animation done in a shader. Only the first LOD flashes. To choose exactly what flashes (skip a weapon, a shadow blob or a VFX mesh), fill in Renderers on Hit Feedback."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your own camera, Cinemachine and camera rigs","u":"hits-integration.html#your-own-camera-cinemachine-and-camera-rigs","x":"Shake moves the camera a little after everything else and puts it back before your scripts run, so camera follow scripts keep working. With Cinemachine 3, tick Use Cinemachine in the profile's shake to send impulses instead (your Cinemachine camera needs an Impulse Listener). When Camera.main is not the camera players see (a rig, several cameras), add Hit Feedback Camera to the right one. Split screen with one shake per player is not supported in 1.0."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"hits-integration.html#multiplayer","x":"Play feedback where it is seen: on every client, from the health value your network code already syncs. Watch that value with the Bridge, or call bridge.HealthChanged(newValue) from your sync callback. Don't send feedback over the network yourself, and don't play it on the server only."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"ScriptableObject event channels","u":"hits-integration.html#scriptableobject-event-channels","x":"Add a listener for your channel on the enemy and point its response at the Bridge's PlayDamage or HealthChanged ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your TextMesh Pro font","u":"hits-integration.html#your-textmesh-pro-font","x":"Add TextMesh Pro Numbers to any object in the scene and drag in your TMP font asset: numbers then use your font and material. See Damage numbers ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Big frameworks and character controllers","u":"hits-integration.html#big-frameworks-and-character-controllers","x":"Most combat frameworks have either a health value (use Watch ) or damage and death events (use events ). If their health lives in a system rather than a field, add a small script that listens to their event and calls the Bridge. Choose the Existing Game preset so knockback and hitstop don't fight their movement. We test against plain Unity components, not against each framework."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"When nothing shows","u":"hits-integration.html#when-nothing-shows","x":"Open Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Feedback? . It lists every hit, says which effects played and why the others didn't, and names objects you hit that have no Hit Feedback. The Hit Feedback inspector also says whether your game has sent it any hits yet (Preview Hit doesn't count)."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Code, events and your own UI","u":"hits-extending.html#code-events-and-your-own-ui","x":"Namespace RareGeometry.ResponsiveFX . Everything below is public and documented in the code."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Hitting","u":"hits-extending.html#hitting","x":"HitFeedback.Hit(target, 25); // GameObject or any Component (a collider you hit) HitFeedback.Hit(target, new HitInfo(25, HitKind.Critical) .At(hitPoint).From(direction).By(attacker).Killing(isKill)); var report = hitFeedback.Play(hitInfo); // what played and why hitFeedback.Freeze(0.1f); // a target-only freeze by itself hitFeedback.StopAll(); // stop everything running on it and put it back"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Events","u":"hits-extending.html#events","x":"Event When HitFeedback.AnyHit (static) Every hit on any target: your combo counter, achievements, logs. hitFeedback.Played Every hit on this target, with its report. OnHit (UnityEvent with the amount), OnKilled In the inspector, no code. Health.OnDamaged , OnHealed , OnDied , Changed Health changes."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your own health","u":"hits-extending.html#your-own-health","x":"public class MyHealth : MonoBehaviour, IHealthSource { public float hp = 100, maxHp = 100; public float CurrentHealth => hp; public float MaxHealth => maxHp; public float CurrentShield => 0; public float MaxShield => 0; }"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your own number display (TextMesh Pro, UI Toolkit)","u":"hits-extending.html#your-own-number-display-textmesh-pro-ui-toolkit","x":"public class MyNumbers : MonoBehaviour, IDamageNumberDisplay { void OnEnable() => DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay = this; void OnDisable() { if (DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay == (IDamageNumberDisplay)this) DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay = null; } public int Show(Vector3 worldPosition, string text, HitKind kind, Color color, float sizeInPixels, Sprite icon, int combineWith, HitFeedback owner) { // Spawn your TextMeshPro popup here. The text is ready: \"1.2K\", \"+25\", \"Miss\". return 0; } } Your own display gets a string per number (Responsive FX's own numbers make none)."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your scripts in hitstop","u":"hits-extending.html#your-scripts-in-hitstop","x":"public class MyAI : MonoBehaviour, IHitstopReceiver { bool frozen; public void OnHitstop(bool isFrozen) => frozen = isFrozen; void Update() { if (frozen) return; /* ... */ } }"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Global settings, time and the screen","u":"hits-extending.html#global-settings-time-and-the-screen","x":"HitFeedbackSettings (accessibility, master juice, volume, rumble, camera, Translate for your localisation), TimeControl.Request(scale, seconds) and StopAll() (safe slow motion), ScreenFlash.Play(color, amount) , DamageNumbers.Show / ShowText / Clear / Hidden / Prewarm , HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible (hide the screen layer behind your menus) and HitCameraShake.For(camera).AddTrauma(amount, settings) ."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Multiplayer","u":"hits-extending.html#multiplayer","x":"Feedback is local to each player's screen, so there is nothing to sync: play the hit on every client from your own networked event (an RPC or a synced health change). With Netcode for GameObjects, call HitFeedback.Hit in the ClientRpc (or OnValueChanged of a health NetworkVariable) that tells clients about the hit."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Performance","u":"hits-extending.html#performance","x":"Numbers, bars and the player's screen effects are drawn in a few batched calls. Hits, numbers and bars make no garbage in steady play (checked by Responsive FX's tests and in built players). Call DamageNumbers.Prewarm() while loading to make the number pool up front."},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","u":"why-no-feedback.html#why-no-feedback-and-scene-overview","x":""},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Feedback?","u":"why-no-feedback.html#why-no-feedback","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Feedback? (also the button in the Hit Feedback inspector). In Play Mode it shows the selected object (or the last one hit) with its eight most recent hits. For each hit: the kind, amount, profile and strength, then every part (number, flash, hitstop, camera shake, knockback, squash, health bar, sound, particles, kill moment) as played or skipped , with the reason in plain words. The latest hit has a one-click fix where there is one: Reason Fix Off on this object Use Profile for that part No camera (tag one MainCamera) Tag the camera as MainCamera No health to sho"},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene Overview","u":"why-no-feedback.html#scene-overview","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Scene Overview lists every Hit Feedback in the open scenes with its preset or profile, what it found (renderers, what pushes it, Animator, health) and any problems, plus the player feedback, screen bars and the camera. Click a name to select it."},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"From code","u":"why-no-feedback.html#from-code","x":"hitFeedback.LastReport , GetReport(i) , HitReport.Reason(effect) , HitReport.Describe(reason) and HitReport.Fix(reason) give you the same information for your own logs or tools."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"hits-samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Open them from Tools > Responsive FX > Demos . They play with keyboard and mouse or a gamepad, with either input system. The Showcase and 2D demos have a Make it yours panel (M or Tab shows and hides it). Every change applies to every enemy at once, including the next wave: a banner names the new look and the enemies on screen take a test hit so you see it straight away. Hit all (or H ) plays a test hit whenever you like. You can change the preset (keys 1 to 7), the number style (Classic, Arcade Punch, Fantasy Relic, Tactical Clean), how numbers show, move and are written, their colour and siz"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Showcase: the arena","u":"hits-samples.html#showcase-the-arena","x":"A knight in a ruined stone ring in the forest: three waves of slimes, then the Grand Gloop with a boss bar. Move with WASD or the left stick, swing your sword with the left mouse button, J or the gamepad's west button. It shows every part of a hit together: numbers that combine, the flash on a skinned slime, hitstop, camera shake and punch, knockback, squash, floating bars, sounds, sparks and the kill moment. When a slime hits you, you see the player side: the red vignette, an arrow pointing at it, your HUD bar draining, and rumble on a gamepad. Keys 1 to 7 switch every enemy to another preset"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Feature Gallery","u":"hits-samples.html#feature-gallery","x":"Seven training dummies, one per preset, hit automatically so you can compare them side by side. Click a dummy to hit it yourself; the panel picks what a click plays (hit, critical, heal, shield, block, miss, poison, a status word, or a killing blow). The accessibility settings (Reduce Motion, flash safety, colour-blind safe colours, text size) and the Juice slider are on the panel too."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"2D Side View","u":"hits-samples.html#2d-side-view","x":"A side-view level with sprites and Rigidbody 2D. A/D or the arrows to move, Space to jump, click, J or the west button to swing. Sprite flash, 2D knockback, squash and stretch on sprites, and the player HUD bar."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"What the demos use","u":"hits-samples.html#what-the-demos-use","x":"Everything in the demos is our own work except the hit sounds, which come from Kenney's Impact Sounds and RPG Audio (CC0; see Third-Party Notices.txt). The demo scripts ( Samples/Scripts ) are examples only: your game uses its own movement, combat and input."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-faq","x":"First, open Why No Feedback? It names the reason for almost everything below, with a fix."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Nothing happens when I hit","u":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-happens-when-i-hit","x":"Is there a Hit Feedback on the object, or a parent of the collider you hit? Why No Feedback? catches hits on objects without one. Using Responsive FX's Health? Damage while invulnerable, or after death, plays nothing. Is HitFeedbackSettings.Enabled on, and the master Juice above 0?"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"No numbers or no shake","u":"troubleshooting.html#no-numbers-or-no-shake","x":"Responsive FX needs a camera: the one tagged MainCamera , or set HitFeedbackSettings.Camera (or the camera tag in Project Settings). Numbers farther than Max Distance aren't shown."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Numbers show over my pause menu / under my UI","u":"troubleshooting.html#numbers-show-over-my-pause-menu--under-my-ui","x":"The screen layer is drawn after the frame. It hides by itself while the game is paused ( Time.timeScale at 0). For other menus set HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible = false while they are open. Your own OnGUI windows draw on top of it."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The flash doesn't show","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-flash-doesnt-show","x":"Static batched objects can't flash: untick Batching Static (Why No Feedback? has the button). Particle, trail and line renderers aren't flashed. Added renderers at runtime? Call RebuildFlash() ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Knockback does nothing","u":"troubleshooting.html#knockback-does-nothing","x":"The object needs something to move: a non-kinematic Rigidbody or Rigidbody 2D, a Character Controller, a NavMesh Agent, or Move Transform turned on. Kinematic bodies are moved like transforms."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"My camera controller and the shake","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-camera-controller-and-the-shake","x":"They don't fight: the shake is added after your controller runs and removed before it runs again. If you shake a camera rig instead, add Hit Camera Shake to the camera and set its Shake Target."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Squash changes my collider","u":"troubleshooting.html#squash-changes-my-collider","x":"Set Hit Feedback > Visual to a child that holds only the model."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Slow motion stayed on","u":"troubleshooting.html#slow-motion-stayed-on","x":"It can't: the game's own time scale always comes back, and if your game changes Time.timeScale (a pause menu) Responsive FX lets go at once. TimeControl.StopAll() ends every stop now."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Is it multiplayer ready?","u":"troubleshooting.html#is-it-multiplayer-ready","x":"Feedback is local; play it on each client from your networked hit event. See Code ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Known limits","u":"troubleshooting.html#known-limits","x":"Numbers and bars are a screen layer, not 3D objects in the scene; \"World\" numbers are sized by distance and hidden behind walls by a raycast. No UI Toolkit or VR-specific feedback in 1.0 (bring your own display through IDamageNumberDisplay )."},{"p":"Footsteps","h":"Footsteps","u":"steps.html#footsteps","x":"Footsteps that sound like what your characters walk on. Add one component to any character and it plays the right sound, effect and footprint for wood, stone, grass, gravel, dirt, mud, sand, leaves, metal, water, carpet, snow, ice or your own surfaces. It works with any character controller, in 3D and 2D, and when a step is silent the Why No Footstep? debugger tells you why in plain words, with a one-click fix."},{"p":"Footsteps","h":"What it does","u":"steps.html#what-it-does","x":"Part What it gives you Surface Footsteps Footsteps on any character: player, NPC, remote player or 2D hero. Walk, run, sprint, crouch, jump and land. Surfaces and the Surface Library Each surface's sounds, effect, footprints and AI loudness, and how Responsive FX recognises it in your scene. Surface Zones Water, puddles and rugs: replace the ground's sound, or add a splash on top. Profiles and presets Realistic, Arcade, Stealth and Heavy feels in one click, then change any value. Effects and footprints Pooled dust, splashes and fading footprints that work in Built-in, URP and HDRP. AI hearing "},{"p":"Footsteps","h":"Where to start","u":"steps.html#where-to-start","x":"Quick start : footsteps on your own character in five minutes. Demo scenes : walk through a house, a garden and a guarded yard. Your controller and game : custom controllers, swimming and ladders, multiplayer, many NPCs. Why No Footstep? : when something sounds wrong."},{"p":"Footsteps","h":"Compatibility","u":"steps.html#compatibility","x":"Unity 6000.0.85f1 and newer Unity 6 versions. Built-in, URP and HDRP. Both input systems (Responsive FX itself reads no input except the debugger's key). No package dependencies."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"steps-quick-start.html#quick-start","x":"Footsteps on your own character in about five minutes. No code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Open the Setup Wizard","u":"steps-quick-start.html#1-open-the-setup-wizard","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Setup Wizard. You can also right-click a character in the Hierarchy and choose Responsive FX > Add Footsteps (Setup Wizard) , or right-click a prefab in the Project window."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Pick your character","u":"steps-quick-start.html#2-pick-your-character","x":"Drag it into Character , or select it and click Use selected . The wizard looks at it and tells you what it found: the controller (Character Controller, Rigidbody, Rigidbody 2D, NavMesh Agent or none) and where the feet are, whether it's the player (tagged Player or has a camera): the player's own steps play flat, the same in both ears, whether its animations already have footstep events, and its foot bones on a humanoid, whether another script already plays footsteps (Unity's Starter Assets do): the wizard can silence it. A prefab is never changed: the wizard saves a Prefab Variant beside it "},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Choose how it feels","u":"steps-quick-start.html#3-choose-how-it-feels","x":"Pick a Preset (Realistic, Arcade, Stealth or Heavy) and where steps come from. Automatic works with every controller: a step every stride while the character moves on the ground."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Map your surfaces","u":"steps-quick-start.html#4-map-your-surfaces","x":"The wizard lists every material, terrain layer and tile in the open scene and what it plays now. It guesses from names (\"Grass_02\" is Grass, \"WoodenBridge\" is Wood). Change any guess, then click Apply . Using the sample library is fine to start; click Make my own copy before you change its sounds, so updates never overwrite your changes."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"5. Add Footsteps and play","u":"steps-quick-start.html#5-add-footsteps-and-play","x":"Click Add Footsteps , then press Play and walk. The wizard checks the setup and lists anything that would stop steps from playing, each with a fix button. No sound, or the wrong one? Open Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Footstep? . It shows every step with its surface and why it was chosen, and the reason for any step it skipped."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Setup Wizard","u":"steps-setup-wizard.html#setup-wizard","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Setup Wizard sets up footsteps on your own character and maps the surfaces in your scene. Nothing changes until you click Add Footsteps or Apply , and each is one undo step."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"1. Your character","u":"steps-setup-wizard.html#1-your-character","x":"What it checks What it does with it Character Controller or Capsule Collider Puts the feet at the bottom of the capsule (the pivot is often in the middle). Rigidbody 2D Picks 2D side view when it has gravity, 2D top-down when it doesn't. No controller Steps follow the object's movement, whatever moves it. Feet go to the bottom of the model. Tagged Player, or has a camera Turns on Flat sound: the player's own steps sound the same in both ears. Humanoid Animator Finds the foot bones, so footprints land under each foot. Footstep animation events Recommends Animation Events for exact timing. Anoth"},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"2. How it feels","u":"steps-setup-wizard.html#2-how-it-feels","x":"Preset: Realistic, Arcade, Stealth or Heavy. See Make it yours . Steps come from: Automatic, Animation Events, Foot Bones or Manual. See When steps happen . Flat sound (the player): on for the character you control, off for NPCs."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"3. Your surfaces","u":"steps-setup-wizard.html#3-your-surfaces","x":"Pick a Surface Library . The sample one has 13 surfaces with sounds: Wood, Stone, Grass, Gravel, Dirt, Mud, Sand, Leaves, Metal, Water, Carpet, Snow and Ice. Make my own copy copies it and its surfaces into Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX , where updates never touch them. The table lists each material, terrain layer and tile in the open scene, how often it's used, and what it plays now (and why). The wizard's guess is already picked; change any, then click Apply to save them in the library as rules. Show selects an object that uses it."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Done","u":"steps-setup-wizard.html#done","x":"After Add Footsteps the wizard runs the setup check and lists any problem with a fix button, for example \"no Audio Listener in the scene\" or \"the ground is on a layer Ground Layers leaves out\"."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","u":"steps-characters.html#surface-footsteps-your-characters","x":"Surface Footsteps goes on each character that should make steps: the player, NPCs, animals, remote players in a multiplayer game. It doesn't move the character and doesn't need to know how it moves."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"The main choices (top of the inspector)","u":"steps-characters.html#the-main-choices-top-of-the-inspector","x":"Setting What it does Library Your surfaces. Profile How steps feel. Empty: the Preset below. New profile from preset makes an asset you can change and share. Steps come from Automatic, Animation Events, Foot Bones or Manual. See When steps happen . Dimension 3D, 2D Side View or 2D Top-Down. See 2D games . A status line under the title says \"Ready\" or the first problem, with Why No Footstep? and Setup Wizard buttons. In Play Mode it shows the last step and Test a step / Test a landing buttons play one on demand."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Ground check and feet","u":"steps-characters.html#ground-check-and-feet","x":"Setting What it does Ground Layers Layers that count as ground. Leave on Everything unless the check hits things it shouldn't. Feet Offset Where the feet are, relative to the pivot. 0 when the pivot is at the feet. Check Height, Check Depth The ground check starts this far above the feet and reaches this far below. Raise the depth for stairs. Foot Spacing Steps and footprints alternate left and right of the centre. Left Foot, Right Foot Bones for the Foot Bones trigger and exact footprints. The wizard finds them on humanoids. Crouch Detection Collider Height (crouched when the capsule shrinks "},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"This character (overrides the profile)","u":"steps-characters.html#this-character-overrides-the-profile","x":"Volume, pitch and stride scales; Footprints, Effects and Noise each Use Profile, On or Off; Spatial (Use Profile, Flat or Full 3D); Forced Surface (a robot always sounds like Metal); Surface Swaps (this giant's Wood sounds different); Gear Sounds played with every step (armour, chains, a squeaky shoe)."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Sound player and events","u":"steps-characters.html#sound-player-and-events","x":"Sound Player: a component that implements IFootstepSoundPlayer plays the sounds instead (FMOD, Wwise, your own audio manager). See Extending . On Step, On Jump, On Land: UnityEvents for no-code wiring (camera bob, controller rumble)."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Swimming, ladders and teleports","u":"steps-characters.html#swimming-ladders-and-teleports","x":"Pause Auto Steps / Resume Auto Steps (also callable from UnityEvents): no automatic steps, jumps or landings while swimming, climbing or in a cutscene. Your own Step() calls still play. ResetMotion() : call it after a teleport, respawn or reusing a pooled character. See Your controller and game for more."},{"p":"Surface Footsteps (your characters)","h":"Moving platforms, stairs, teleports","u":"steps-characters.html#moving-platforms-stairs-teleports","x":"Standing still on a moving platform plays nothing: Responsive FX takes the platform's movement out. Stairs work with the default check depth; raise Check Depth for tall steps. A jump of more than 4 m in one frame is never a step."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"When steps happen","u":"steps-triggers.html#when-steps-happen","x":"Steps come from on Surface Footsteps decides when a step plays."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Automatic (default)","u":"steps-triggers.html#automatic-default","x":"A step every stride while the character moves on the ground. The stride is set per movement in the profile (walk 0.75 m, run 1.15 m, sprint 1.4 m, crouch 0.55 m in Realistic) and the movement comes from the speed (run from 3.5 m/s, sprint from 6.5 m/s). Works with every controller, NPCs moved by any script, and remote players, because it only watches movement. Jumps and landings are detected the same way: leaving the ground upwards faster than Jump Speed is a jump, touching down after Min Air Time is a landing, louder for a faster fall."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Animation Events","u":"steps-triggers.html#animation-events","x":"For exact timing with animated characters. Add events to your walk and run clips with one of these function names: Footstep , FootstepLeft , FootstepRight , FootL , FootR , OnFootstep (the name Unity's Starter Assets and many animation packs already use), and OnLand for landings. Unity sends animation events to the Animator's own object. When Surface Footsteps sits on a parent (usual), add Footstep Animation Events to the Animator's object; the Setup Wizard and the debugger's fix button do it for you. In a blend tree or crossfade every clip fires its events. An event from a clip with half the "},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Foot Bones","u":"steps-triggers.html#foot-bones","x":"For animated characters without events: Responsive FX watches each foot bone and plays a step when it comes down. It learns how high each foot lifts, so it works with any rig and scale. Set Left Foot and Right Foot (the wizard finds them on humanoids)."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Manual","u":"steps-triggers.html#manual","x":"Only when your code calls Step() , Step(Foot, FootstepMovement) , Jump() or Land(fallSpeed) . Use it for cutscenes, ladders or anything with its own timing."},{"p":"When steps happen","h":"Never machine-gun steps","u":"steps-triggers.html#never-machine-gun-steps","x":"Min Step Interval (0.12 s in Realistic) is the shortest time between two steps, whatever triggers them. Skipped steps show \"Too soon\" in the debugger."},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","u":"steps-surfaces.html#surfaces-and-the-surface-library","x":""},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Surfaces","u":"steps-surfaces.html#surfaces","x":"A Surface (Assets > Create > Responsive FX > Surface) is one kind of ground: Wood, Grass, Metal, your own \"Squeaky Floorboards\". It holds: Setting What it does Display Name, Colour Shown in the debugger, gizmos and Scene Overview. Keywords Words that find this surface in material, texture, terrain layer, tile and object names (grass, lawn, turf). Walk, Run, Crouch, Jump, Land A sound set each. Run, Crouch and Land fall back to Walk when empty; Jump plays nothing when empty. Volume Volume of everything on this surface. Step Effect, Land Effect Optional prefabs (dust, splashes), pooled. Footprin"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Your own sounds in one click","u":"steps-surfaces.html#your-own-sounds-in-one-click","x":"Select your footstep clips (or a whole folder of them) in the Project window and choose Assets > Responsive FX > Make Surface From Selected Clips . It makes a new surface in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX/Surfaces : Clips with run , sprint or jog in their name go to Run, crouch or sneak to Crouch, jump to Jump and land to Land; everything else is a walk step. The surface is named after the word the clip names share (\"Gravel_Step_01\", \"Gravel_Run_02\" makes \"Gravel\"), and that word becomes its keyword, so materials called \"Gravel...\" find it straight away. It's added to your own Surface Li"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Sounds included","u":"steps-surfaces.html#sounds-included","x":"The sample library has 13 surfaces and more than 200 sounds, CC0 recordings plus a few of our own (see Third-Party Notices.txt): Wood, Stone, Grass, Gravel, Dirt, Mud, Sand, Leaves, Metal, Water, Carpet, Snow and Ice, with landing sounds for the soft surfaces, wood, metal and water. There are also gear sounds a character can add to every step (Leather, Cloth, Plate Armour, Armour Jingle) and a Robot Feet surface to force on a robot. Make your own copy (Setup Wizard) before changing them."},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"The Surface Library","u":"steps-surfaces.html#the-surface-library","x":"A Surface Library (Create > Responsive FX > Surface Library) lists your surfaces and how to recognise them. Given what a foot touched, it checks in this order and stops at the first answer: A Surface Zone the foot is inside (water, a rug). See Zones . A Surface Tag on the object or a parent: the surest way to say \"this is Metal\". Another tool's surface tag: any component with a public text property called SurfaceName that names a surface here (a level builder's Surface Tag works this way, no setup). A terrain layer under the foot (with Blend Two, the second layer joins in quieter where they mi"},{"p":"Surfaces and the Surface Library","h":"Tips","u":"steps-surfaces.html#tips","x":"Already have physics materials per ground? Map those once and every object using them is covered. Painted terrain at runtime? Call library.RefreshTerrain(terrainData) . Changed a material from code? Call SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump() ."},{"p":"Surface Zones","h":"Surface Zones","u":"surface-zones.html#surface-zones","x":"A Surface Zone changes the steps inside an area: shallow water, a puddle, a rug on a wooden floor, a snowy patch on a road. Create one with GameObject > Responsive FX > Surface Zone (a trigger box), or add the component to any object with a collider. The collider is the zone's shape; it can be a trigger. Setting What it does Surface The surface inside. Mode Replace : only the zone plays (wading in a pond, a rug). Add : the ground plays and the zone plays on top (a puddle on stone splashes and still sounds like stone). Priority When zones overlap, the highest wins. Added Volume The zone's volum"},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Effects and footprints","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#effects-and-footprints","x":""},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Step effects","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#step-effects","x":"Give a surface a Step Effect prefab (and optionally a Land Effect ): dust on gravel, splashes in water, bits of grass. Any prefab works; particle systems in it are restarted each time. Effects are pooled (up to 16 copies of each), so steps never create objects or garbage. Landings scale the effect up for a harder fall. The profile's Effect Scale sizes them all; Show Effects turns them off. The samples include Dust Puff, Grass Bits, Splash and Snow Puff, drawn with Responsive FX's own shader."},{"p":"Effects and footprints","h":"Footprints","u":"effects-and-footprints.html#footprints","x":"Footprints appear on surfaces with Footprints ticked (Snow, Gravel and Grass in the sample library), in that surface's Footprint Colour , when the profile's Show Footprints is on and it has a Footprint Picture . They're pooled ( Max Footprints , oldest reused), stay for Lifetime seconds and fade over Fade seconds. Each print follows the ground's slope and the direction of travel; left and right alternate (the left is the picture mirrored). With foot bones set, prints land exactly under each foot. Landings leave both feet. Footprints use Responsive FX's Unlit Transparent shader, which has Built"},{"p":"AI hearing (stealth)","h":"AI hearing (stealth)","u":"ai-hearing.html#ai-hearing-stealth","x":"Every step can be heard by AI. How far it carries is the profile's noise distance for the movement (walk 6 m, run 10 m, sprint 14 m, crouch 1.5 m, landing 12 m in Realistic) times the surface's Loudness (soft grass 0.6, metal 1.6) times the character's volume. Crouching on grass is nearly silent; sprinting on metal carries far."},{"p":"AI hearing (stealth)","h":"Footstep Listener","u":"ai-hearing.html#footstep-listener","x":"Add Footstep Listener to a guard: Setting What it does Hearing 1 normal, 2 hears twice as far, 0 deaf. Walls Muffle A step behind something solid carries only half as far. Wall Layers What counts as a wall. Ignore Own Steps The guard doesn't hear its own feet. On Heard A UnityEvent with the step's position: turn, investigate, raise the alarm. From code, Heard gives the whole step (who, where, which surface, how loud), and LastHeardPosition , LastHeardTime and LastHeardDistance01 are always available. Your own AI can listen to every step without a component: FootstepNoise.Made += step => ... . "},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D games","u":"steps-2d.html#2d-games","x":"Set Dimension on Surface Footsteps (the Setup Wizard picks it from a Rigidbody 2D)."},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D Side View (platformers)","u":"steps-2d.html#2d-side-view-platformers","x":"The ground check is a 2D ray down from the feet. Tilemaps with a Tilemap Collider 2D play each tile's surface: map tiles (or their sprites) to surfaces in the wizard or Scene Overview, or name them with a keyword (\"GrassTop\"). Other 2D colliders work like 3D ones: Surface Tags, physics materials, sprite materials and names. Zones use 2D colliders. Footprints are off in side view (there's no floor to print on)."},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D Top-Down","u":"steps-2d.html#2d-top-down","x":"The floor is whatever is under the character's position: 2D colliders there (triggers too, for floor areas), then the Floor Tilemaps you list (top layer last), then the default surface. Footprints lie flat facing the camera and point the way the character moves."},{"p":"2D games","h":"Sound","u":"steps-2d.html#sound","x":"2D games usually want flat sound: set Spatial to Flat on the player, or Spatial Blend 0 in the profile. The 2D Side View demo walks across grass, wood, stone, metal, snow, carpet and gravel tiles into a pool."},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","u":"steps-make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours-profiles-and-presets","x":"Everything a player hears, sees or that an AI reacts to can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Presets first","u":"steps-make-it-yours.html#presets-first","x":"A Footstep Profile sets how steps feel. Start from a preset, then change any value: Preset Feel Realistic Natural strides, gentle variation, subtle dust and prints. Arcade Snappy and punchy: louder, bigger puffs, longer-lasting prints. Stealth Quiet: very soft crouching, and noise matters more to AI. Heavy Big, slow characters: long strides, low pitch, heavy landings. Create one with Create > Responsive FX > Footstep Profile or the New profile from preset button on Surface Footsteps. The profile inspector has a Preset picker and Apply preset at the top (one undo step). Without a profile asset,"},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Everything in a profile","u":"steps-make-it-yours.html#everything-in-a-profile","x":"Group Settings Volume and pitch Volume, pitch, pitch variation, walk / run / sprint / crouch volume. Stride Walk, run, sprint and crouch stride; run and sprint speeds; minimum speed; min step interval. Jump and landing Play jump, play land, jump speed, min air time, soft and hard landing speeds, landing volume. Effects Show effects, effect scale. Footprints Show footprints, footprint picture, material, size, lifetime, fade, maximum at once. 3D sound Spatial blend, min and max distance, rolloff, Audio Mixer group, priority. Noise for AI Emit noise; how far walk, run, sprint, crouch and landing "},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Per character","u":"steps-make-it-yours.html#per-character","x":"Each Surface Footsteps can override its profile: volume, pitch and stride scales, footprints / effects / noise on or off, flat or 3D sound, a forced surface, surface swaps and gear sounds. See Surface Footsteps ."},{"p":"Make it yours: profiles and presets","h":"Your own sounds, pictures and audio system","u":"steps-make-it-yours.html#your-own-sounds-pictures-and-audio-system","x":"Every sound is a clip you choose, per surface and movement. Every effect is your own prefab; footprints use your own picture (a right foot pointing up; the left is mirrored). Bring your own audio: put a component implementing IFootstepSoundPlayer in Sound Player and Responsive FX hands it every step (FMOD, Wwise, your manager). See Extending . Switch anything off: no effects, no footprints, no AI noise, no jump sound, or mute a surface. The demos' Make it yours panel (M) switches presets, footprints, effects, flat or 3D sound and volume while you play."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Your controller and game","u":"your-game.html#your-controller-and-game","x":"Responsive FX doesn't move your character and doesn't need to know how it moves, so it fits most games as they are. This page covers the cases that need one extra setting or one line of code."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Which \"Steps come from\" to pick","u":"your-game.html#which-steps-come-from-to-pick","x":"Your character Pick Why First person, or any controller without foot animation Automatic A step every stride from the movement. Nothing to set up. Animated, and its clips already have footstep events (Starter Assets, many animation packs) Animation Events Steps land exactly when the foot does. Animated humanoid without events Foot Bones Responsive FX watches each foot come down. Special movement with its own timing (a mech, a horse, network events) Manual Your code calls Step() , Jump() and Land() ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Any character controller","u":"your-game.html#any-character-controller","x":"Put Surface Footsteps on the object that moves, or on the model under it. When it sits on the model and the controller is on a parent (a Character Controller, or a Rigidbody with a capsule), Responsive FX treats the whole character as its own body: none of its colliders count as ground. Most controller packs (and Unity's Starter Assets) can play footsteps of their own. Turn those off , or every step is heard twice. The Setup Wizard silences Starter Assets for you, and Why No Footstep? warns when another script on the character looks like it plays footsteps. Walk, run and sprint come from the s"},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Swimming, ladders, sliding, riding, cutscenes","u":"your-game.html#swimming-ladders-sliding-riding-cutscenes","x":"Call Pause Auto Steps when the special movement starts and Resume Auto Steps when it ends. Both are in the UnityEvent list, so a trigger or your controller's events can call them without code: footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(true); // into the water, onto the ladder footsteps.Step(Foot.Left, FootstepMovement.Walk); // your own steps still play (a rung, a stroke) footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(false); // back on the ground: no stray landing"},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Teleports, respawns and pooled characters","u":"your-game.html#teleports-respawns-and-pooled-characters","x":"A jump of more than 4 m in one frame is never a step. For anything shorter (a respawn nearby, a pooled NPC reused), call footsteps.ResetMotion() right after moving it. Disabling and enabling the component resets it too."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Pause and slow motion","u":"your-game.html#pause-and-slow-motion","x":"With Time.timeScale at 0 nothing plays. Slow motion slows the steps with the movement, because steps follow distance. Responsive FX's sounds are normal Audio Sources, so AudioListener.pause pauses them like the rest of your game's sound, and the profile's Audio Mixer Group sends them to your mixer."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Your own audio system (FMOD, Wwise, an audio manager)","u":"your-game.html#your-own-audio-system-fmod-wwise-an-audio-manager","x":"Put a component that implements IFootstepSoundPlayer in Sound Player . Responsive FX still finds the surface, effects, footprints and AI noise, and hands each sound to your component instead of playing it. See Extending ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"your-game.html#multiplayer","x":"Responsive FX has no network code and needs none. Pick one of these per character, not both: Every client plays steps from what it sees (simplest): put Surface Footsteps on every player and NPC with Automatic. Remote players' movement plays their steps on each client. Steps follow the owner's events: use Manual on remote copies and call Step() when your network event arrives. Give the local player Spatial: Flat and remote players Full 3D ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Split screen","u":"your-game.html#split-screen","x":"Unity hears through one Audio Listener. With two players on one screen, use 3D sound with a generous Max Distance in the profile, or play local players' steps flat."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Many NPCs","u":"your-game.html#many-npcs","x":"Each character does one short ground check a frame and makes up to four Audio Sources when it first steps. For crowds: turn off Effects and Footprints on background NPCs (This character: Off), use a profile with a smaller Max Distance and a lower Priority for NPCs than for the player, disable Surface Footsteps on NPCs far from the camera, and enable it again when they come close."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Terrain","u":"your-game.html#terrain","x":"Terrain layers are read once, when the scene starts, in small pieces so even big terrains don't hitch. Several terrains side by side work: each step reads the terrain it hit, and holes are respected. Painted at runtime? Call library.RefreshTerrain(terrainData) . Loaded a terrain at runtime? Call library.PrewarmTerrains() . Terrain shaders that blend by vertex colour or inside the shader (some MicroSplat and Polybrush setups) can't be read from the terrain's layers. Use Surface Zones for those areas, Surface Tags on objects, or map the terrain's layers that still exist."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Levels built at runtime and Addressables","u":"your-game.html#levels-built-at-runtime-and-addressables","x":"Nothing needs to be set up in advance: the first step on a new collider works out its surface, and the answer is remembered. If you change a material or tag from code, call SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump() ."},{"p":"Your controller and game","h":"Ask Responsive FX what a surface is","u":"your-game.html#ask-responsive-fx-what-a-surface-is","x":"Bullets, dropped items, tyres: use the same answer a footstep gets, zones included. if (library.TryResolveAt(position, out var result)) PlayImpact(result.surface); var fromHit = library.ResolveHit(hit);"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Extending with code","u":"steps-extending.html#extending-with-code","x":"Everything here is optional: Responsive FX needs no code. Namespace RareGeometry.ResponsiveFX ."},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Listen to steps","u":"steps-extending.html#listen-to-steps","x":"var footsteps = GetComponent<SurfaceFootsteps>(); footsteps.Stepped += step => { // step.surface.surface: the Surface; step.surface.source: why it was chosen // step.point, step.normal, step.foot, step.movement (Walk, Run, Sprint, Crouch, Jump, Land) // step.volume, step.intensity (landings: how hard), step.noiseRadius, step.addedZone }; footsteps.Attempted += record => { /* every attempt, with record.result when it didn't play */ };"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Play steps yourself","u":"steps-extending.html#play-steps-yourself","x":"footsteps.Trigger = FootstepTrigger.Manual; footsteps.Step(); // the current movement, alternating feet footsteps.Step(Foot.Left, FootstepMovement.Run); footsteps.Jump(); footsteps.Land(fallSpeed: 7f); footsteps.SetCrouching(true); footsteps.SetAutoStepsPaused(true); // swimming, ladders, cutscenes (Step() still works) footsteps.ResetMotion(); // after a teleport or respawn"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Your own audio system","u":"steps-extending.html#your-own-audio-system","x":"public sealed class FmodFootsteps : MonoBehaviour, IFootstepSoundPlayer { public void PlayFootstep(in FootstepEvent step, AudioClip clip, float volume, float pitch) { // Play your event with step.surface.surface.DisplayName as a parameter, at step.point. } } Drag the component into Sound Player on Surface Footsteps. Responsive FX then plays nothing itself."},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Ask what a surface is","u":"steps-extending.html#ask-what-a-surface-is","x":"SurfaceResolution r = library.ResolveHit(hit); // zones first, then the collider, like a footstep if (library.TryResolveAt(position, out var below)) { } // cast down from a point, no character needed if (r.Found) Debug.Log(r.surface.DisplayName + \" \" + FootstepDiagnostics.SourceWords(r)); library.SetRule(myMaterial, metalSurface); // map from code library.RefreshTerrain(terrain.terrainData); // after painting at runtime SurfaceCacheVersion.Bump(); // after changing materials or tags from code library.PrewarmTerrains(); // after loading a terrain at runtime (no hitch on the first step)"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"AI hearing","u":"steps-extending.html#ai-hearing","x":"FootstepNoise.Made += step => { if (step.noiseRadius > Vector3.Distance(step.point, transform.position)) Investigate(step.point); };"},{"p":"Extending with code","h":"Setup checks from code","u":"steps-extending.html#setup-checks-from-code","x":"var problems = new List<FootstepProblem>(); FootstepDiagnostics.Check(footsteps, problems); // the same checks as the debugger window Responsive FX has no static state that survives Play Mode, so it works with Domain Reload disabled."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","u":"why-no-footstep.html#why-no-footstep-and-scene-overview","x":""},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Footstep? (window)","u":"why-no-footstep.html#why-no-footstep-window","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Footstep? Pick a character (or select it in the Hierarchy). Setup check lists every problem it finds, most serious first, each with a fix button where one exists: Problem One-click fix No Surface Library Use the sample library (or yours) The library has no default surface Set a default A surface has no Walk sounds Select the surface The ground is on a layer Ground Layers leaves out (\"Floor is on layer Environment\") Include that layer The feet are below or above the ground (\"feet are 0.90 m below the ground\") Put the feet on the ground The ground matches no surfac"},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"In-game panel","u":"why-no-footstep.html#in-game-panel","x":"In-game panel adds a Footstep Debug Overlay to the scene: the same live view inside the Game view and in development builds. F4 shows and hides it (change the key, the corner and the rows in its inspector). With Development Only on (the default) it never shows in a release build."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene View","u":"why-no-footstep.html#scene-view","x":"Select a character to see its ground check (blue) and the last steps: a line and dot per step in the surface's colour, red for a step that didn't play."},{"p":"Why No Footstep? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene Overview","u":"why-no-footstep.html#scene-overview","x":"Tools > Responsive FX > Scene Overview shows the whole scene: Characters: each one with its library, profile and any problems (with fixes). Surfaces: every material, terrain layer and tile, what it plays now and why, with a picker to map it. Zones: every zone, its surface and mode; adds a shape to a zone without one."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"steps-samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Open them from Tools > Responsive FX > Demos . Controls: WASD or the left stick to move, Shift to run (hold longer to sprint), C or Ctrl to crouch, Space to jump. Click the game to look around with the mouse; Escape frees the mouse. M shows the Make it yours panel, P changes the feel, F turns footprints on and off, F4 shows the Why No Footstep? panel."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Showcase","u":"steps-samples.html#showcase","x":"A walk from a wooden house (with a carpet) out to a stone patio and a garden: a terrain painted with grass, a gravel path, a snowy corner, a mud patch and fallen leaves. Cross the pond on the wooden bridge or wade through it, climb the metal stairs, then sneak past the guard in the gravel yard: crouch and it can't hear you, sprint and it turns to look. A knight NPC patrols with heavy steps and plate armour, and a robot patrols on its own metal feet whatever the ground (a Forced Surface); both are moved by a plain script that knows nothing about footsteps."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Feature Gallery","u":"steps-samples.html#feature-gallery","x":"One pad of each of the 13 surfaces, a ledge to jump off for landings, a moving platform (standing still on it plays no steps), and a puddle that adds splashes on top of the stone. The Make it yours panel switches presets, footprints, effects, 3D or flat sound and volume while you play."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"2D Side View","u":"steps-samples.html#2d-side-view","x":"A platformer level made of tiles: grass, wood, stone, metal, sand, mud, snow, ice, carpet and gravel. Each tile plays its own surface; the pool at the end splashes. The character uses Surface Footsteps set to 2D Side View. All demo content is generated, uses only what ships, and works in Built-in, URP and HDRP."},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Troubleshooting","u":"steps-troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting","x":"Start with Tools > Responsive FX > Why No Footstep? : it checks the setup and explains every step. What you see Why, and what to do No steps at all Is there an Audio Listener in the scene? Is the character moving faster than Min Speed (0.25 m/s)? The window's Live view shows both. \"No ground\" in the debugger The ground check misses the ground: the feet are in the wrong place (Feet Offset), the check is too short (Check Depth), or the ground's layer isn't in Ground Layers. The setup check finds which and fixes it. The wrong surface plays The debugger says why each surface was chosen. Map the ma"},{"p":"Troubleshooting","h":"Known issues","u":"steps-troubleshooting.html#known-issues","x":"None yet. Found one? See Support ."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"upgrading.html#100","x":"First release. Nothing to do."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Updating Responsive FX later","u":"upgrading.html#updating-responsive-fx-later","x":"Import the new version over the old one. Your profiles, settings and Setup Wizard variants live in Assets/RareGeometryData/ResponsiveFX , which updates never touch. Class names, serialized fields, menu paths and settings keys don't change between versions, so your scenes, prefabs and code keep working. Anything that needs a step from you is listed here. If you moved or renamed Responsive FX's folder, that's fine: the tools find Responsive FX's assets by name, not by path."},{"p":"Help and support","h":"Help and support","u":"help-in-unity.html#help-and-support","x":"In Unity: Tools > Responsive FX > Help and Support shows the version, the quick start, the demos and every support address as text you can copy. Copy system info there copies the Responsive FX version, Unity version, render pipeline and platform; paste it into your message so we can see your setup. Tools > Responsive FX > Diagnostics lists anything in the open scenes that would stop a hit or a step from responding, each with a fix."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":""},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0]","u":"changelog.html#100","x":"First release. Impact Lab : surfaces against contact kinds in one grid, pairings (a blade on wood), the Event Trace with reasons, test buttons in Edit Mode and Play Mode, player option rules. Surfaces and responses : 17 sample surfaces with answers for steps, landings, hits, bullets, bumps, doors, slides, rolls and breaks; weather conditions (wet, snowy) and footwear. Hit feedback : numbers, flash, hitstop, shake, knockback, squash, health bars, sounds, sparks and the kill moment; genre presets, a Juice slider, Hit Recipe Studio; four looks that restyle numbers, bars, flash and sparks together"},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Stuck, or found a bug in the Responsive FX? Every message is read by the developer who wrote it."},{"p":"Support","h":"Where to ask","u":"support.html#where-to-ask","x":"Discord (questions, ideas, show and tell): https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu Bug tracker (report a bug and follow it until it's fixed): https://github.com/raregeometry/Support Email : raregeometrysupport@gmail.com Website and documentation : https://raregeometry.github.io"},{"p":"Support","h":"Reporting a bug","u":"support.html#reporting-a-bug","x":"Send these and it can be fixed fast: The support info. In Tools > Responsive FX > Help and Support , click Copy system info : it puts the kit's version, Unity's version and your render pipeline on the clipboard. Nothing is sent anywhere. What you did , step by step, and what you expected to happen. What happened instead , with a screenshot if it shows. Any red errors from the Console (click one, then copy its full text)."}];
